So they want to just
transfer and chat

00:12:57.080 --> 00:13:01.310
with a live agent on that
specific chat via text.

00:13:01.310 --> 00:13:03.663
How does your
platform handle that?

00:13:03.663 --> 00:13:04.580
EDWIN MARGULIES: Sure.

00:13:04.580 --> 00:13:06.320
Well, based on that
scenario, a customer

00:13:06.320 --> 00:13:08.840
can escalate to a
human-based chat

00:13:08.840 --> 00:13:11.762
when they're done with
Google Dialogflow.

00:13:11.762 --> 00:13:13.220
Essentially, all
the customer needs

00:13:13.220 --> 00:13:16.220
to do is type the word agent
or give me a live person

00:13:16.220 --> 00:13:20.420
or something related to that, or
click on the live agent avatar.

00:13:20.420 --> 00:13:23.860
Then we automatically engage the
Thrio chat server in the Thrio

00:13:23.860 --> 00:13:25.770
application cluster.

00:13:25.770 --> 00:13:28.650
Next, we do a database
lookup in Google BigQuery

00:13:28.650 --> 00:13:32.040
and also check the intelligent
router for available agents

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:33.030
to connect to--

00:13:33.030 --> 00:13:34.860
that is, the ones
who are properly

00:13:34.860 --> 00:13:37.860
skilled to help that
particular customer.

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:40.260
And we use Google
Cloud AutoML text

00:13:40.260 --> 00:13:43.290
to help with the
classification so we can then

00:13:43.290 --> 00:13:46.890
do the automated routing in
a very quick and professional

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:47.950
way.

00:13:47.950 --> 00:13:51.390
Finally, the Thrio chat server
in the Thrio application

00:13:51.390 --> 00:13:55.050
cluster sets up a
channel with a live agent

00:13:55.050 --> 00:13:57.930
after notifying the agent
of the chat request.

00:13:57.930 --> 00:14:01.800
And then information from the
Google Dialogflow bot session

00:14:01.800 --> 00:14:05.340
is sent to the agent's screen
so the agent can continue

00:14:05.340 --> 00:14:07.890
the dialogue with the
customer while seeing

00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:10.560
the context of the previous
session with the bot,

00:14:10.560 --> 00:14:12.870
therefore eliminating
the need for the customer

00:14:12.870 --> 00:14:15.090
to have to repeat themselves.

00:14:15.090 --> 00:14:16.380
FILLIPO MADELLA: OK.

00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:18.990
The last part now
that I'm curious,

00:14:18.990 --> 00:14:22.930
now you're talking to this
human agent on the chat.

00:14:22.930 --> 00:14:26.610
And then, for some reason,
you need to go from the chat

00:14:26.610 --> 00:14:28.710
now to a phone call.

00:14:28.710 --> 00:14:29.580
Is that possible?

00:14:29.580 --> 00:14:32.880
And, if so, how is that
handled in the backend?

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:35.280
EDWIN MARGULIES: OK, well,
that would make for the second

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:38.400
cross-channel escalation
in this customer journey--

00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:40.800
the first one being the
escalation from the Dialogflow

00:14:40.800 --> 00:14:43.350
bot to a live agent
chat, and the second

00:14:43.350 --> 00:14:45.340
being a switch
from chat to phone.

00:14:45.340 --> 00:14:47.460
But we can handle all
of that, not a problem.

00:14:47.460 --> 00:14:50.040
In this case, the agent views
the customer contact record

00:14:50.040 --> 00:14:51.870
and sees the
customer's phone number

00:14:51.870 --> 00:14:53.700
retrieved from Google BigQuery.

00:14:53.700 --> 00:14:55.950
Now the agent can
terminate the chat

00:14:55.950 --> 00:14:58.140
and simply click
on the phone number

00:14:58.140 --> 00:15:00.900
with her mouse to
call the customer.

00:15:00.900 --> 00:15:03.720
In reaction to that
click made by the agent,

00:15:03.720 --> 00:15:06.360
the media controllers in
the application cluster

00:15:06.360 --> 00:15:09.900
send call setup information
to the telephony cluster,

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:11.790
which, in turn,
establishes a phone call

00:15:11.790 --> 00:15:15.750
leg with the customer via
regular telephone network.

00:15:15.750 --> 00:15:19.590
And then the agent is patched
into the call via WebRTC--

00:15:19.590 --> 00:15:23.617
that is, a WebRTC
connector in Google Chrome.

00:15:23.617 --> 00:15:24.450
FILLIPO MADELLA: OK.

00:15:24.450 --> 00:15:29.890
And I'm assuming when you're
having these conversations,

00:15:29.890 --> 00:15:33.130
I know that they're most
likely being transcripted

00:15:33.130 --> 00:15:35.350
to be either saved
on your system

00:15:35.350 --> 00:15:39.490
or as part of your
analytics workflows.

00:15:39.490 --> 00:15:42.970
So is that something that
your platform does today,

00:15:42.970 --> 00:15:46.897
and are you using any of
our solutions to do that?

00:15:46.897 --> 00:15:47.980
EDWIN MARGULIES: Oh, sure.

00:15:47.980 --> 00:15:51.400
Well, an agent or a
Google Dialogflow IVR

00:15:51.400 --> 00:15:54.550
can ask permission to record
a call for quality purposes.

00:15:54.550 --> 00:15:57.680
And, with that permission,
recordings are enabled.

00:15:57.680 --> 00:16:00.010
So in this case, an agent
activates the recording

00:16:00.010 --> 00:16:03.190
with a button in the
Thrio UI using Chrome,

00:16:03.190 --> 00:16:06.550
and this sends a signal to the
Google application cluster,

00:16:06.550 --> 00:16:08.680
which in turn tells
the telephony cluster

00:16:08.680 --> 00:16:10.120
to begin recording.

00:16:10.120 --> 00:16:14.510
Afterwards, the recording is
stored in Google Cloud Storage.

00:16:14.510 --> 00:16:17.900
Also, the Thrio Workflow engine
in the application cluster

00:16:17.900 --> 00:16:21.500
causes the recording to be
streamed to the Google AI

00:16:21.500 --> 00:16:25.880
cluster so we can call upon
Google Cloud Speech to Text.

00:16:25.880 --> 00:16:27.650
The resulting
transcription of the call

00:16:27.650 --> 00:16:30.920
is also sent to
Google Cloud Storage.

00:16:30.920 --> 00:16:34.010
FILLIPO MADELLA: And do
you do any type of analysis

00:16:34.010 --> 00:16:34.940
on these calls?

00:16:34.940 --> 00:16:39.680
For example, if there's any
concerns within the text,

00:16:39.680 --> 00:16:41.900
like you want to
evaluate the quality,

00:16:41.900 --> 00:16:45.170
or you want to escalate
to a supervisor

00:16:45.170 --> 00:16:47.540
based on what was
said on the call,

00:16:47.540 --> 00:16:51.350
do you do any sort of
analysis on what was said?

00:16:51.350 --> 00:16:52.490
EDWIN MARGULIES: Oh, sure.

00:16:52.490 --> 00:16:55.250
In fact, based on the parameters
of a customer journey,

00:16:55.250 --> 00:16:57.380
the Google Cloud
Speech to Text is

00:16:57.380 --> 00:17:00.380
used to take the transcribed
text and stream it

00:17:00.380 --> 00:17:04.490
to Google AutoML Natural
Language for analysis

00:17:04.490 --> 00:17:06.380
of compliance phrases.

00:17:06.380 --> 00:17:11.099
The results are then returned to
the Thrio application cluster.

00:17:11.099 --> 00:17:12.690
And supervisors
can get involved,

00:17:12.690 --> 00:17:15.000
because the User Service
in the application cluster

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:18.180
sends a live agent
dashboard alarm

00:17:18.180 --> 00:17:21.510
to the supervisor indicating
that the agent missed

00:17:21.510 --> 00:17:24.510
a compliance phrase while
talking with the customer.

00:17:24.510 --> 00:17:28.260
The supervisor then reviews the
transcript and sees the misses.

00:17:28.260 --> 00:17:30.840
Now the supervisor can
start a one-on-one chat

00:17:30.840 --> 00:17:34.110
with the agent to coach
the agent on the compliance

00:17:34.110 --> 00:17:35.830
phrases that were missed.

00:17:35.830 --> 00:17:38.610
And this is set up using our
chat service in the application

00:17:38.610 --> 00:17:41.130
cluster and company directory.

00:17:41.130 --> 00:17:43.500
Look, this is just one of
hundreds of flexible customer

00:17:43.500 --> 00:17:45.960
journeys that can be
achieved using Thrio.

00:17:45.960 --> 00:17:48.120
FILLIPO MADELLA:
OK, well, thank you.

00:17:48.120 --> 00:17:51.510
I'm curious now
on the data piece.

00:17:51.510 --> 00:17:53.340
Like I mentioned,
this is something

00:17:53.340 --> 00:17:58.770
that I'm imagining you
are processing hundreds

00:17:58.770 --> 00:18:00.360
of thousands, if not millions.

00:18:00.360 --> 00:18:05.520
You mentioned you have a
global scale, global reach.

00:18:05.520 --> 00:18:09.060
So what do you do with all
the information, not just

00:18:09.060 --> 00:18:13.080
the calls, but the
metadata from these calls--

00:18:13.080 --> 00:18:14.503
what do you do today with that?

00:18:14.503 --> 00:18:15.420
EDWIN MARGULIES: Sure.

00:18:15.420 --> 00:18:19.470
Well, we use Google BigQuery
to house all transaction

00:18:19.470 --> 00:18:20.700
histories.

00:18:20.700 --> 00:18:22.740
It's used for
analytics, dashboards,

00:18:22.740 --> 00:18:25.680
special integrations
with other systems.

00:18:25.680 --> 00:18:29.340
BigQuery has helped tremendously
with Thrio partners,

00:18:29.340 --> 00:18:34.650
such as larger service providers
and BPOs who need direct access

00:18:34.650 --> 00:18:36.180
to their own data.

00:18:36.180 --> 00:18:39.300
In these larger service
provider scenarios,

00:18:39.300 --> 00:18:41.820
a Thrio partner may
have their own instance

00:18:41.820 --> 00:18:43.920
of Google BigQuery running.

00:18:43.920 --> 00:18:46.410
The partner will give
Thrio permissions,

00:18:46.410 --> 00:18:49.140
via Google Security
Token Service,

00:18:49.140 --> 00:18:51.570
to read and write to
the database-- that is,

00:18:51.570 --> 00:18:52.980
their database.

00:18:52.980 --> 00:18:55.380
Customers are able to run
their own queries that

00:18:55.380 --> 00:18:56.920
are unique to their business.

00:18:56.920 --> 00:18:59.640
And, in addition, they
can run the canned reports

00:18:59.640 --> 00:19:01.740
that Thrio has written, as well.

00:19:01.740 --> 00:19:02.850
FILLIPO MADELLA: Wow.

00:19:02.850 --> 00:19:04.770
It's a very unique
use case, where

00:19:04.770 --> 00:19:07.980
you're allowing your customers,
almost like using this Titanic

00:19:07.980 --> 00:19:12.030
scenario with BigQuery, allowing
them to query their own data.

00:19:12.030 --> 00:19:17.160
Did you have any challenges
implementing the solution,

00:19:17.160 --> 00:19:18.613
something that you could share?

00:19:18.613 --> 00:19:19.530
EDWIN MARGULIES: Sure.

00:19:19.530 --> 00:19:21.810
Well, at first,
the extent to which

00:19:21.810 --> 00:19:24.300
customers would be
running their own queries

00:19:24.300 --> 00:19:28.260
would affect other customers,
because the spirit of CCaaS

00:19:28.260 --> 00:19:31.020
is that it's a shared
tenancy arrangement where

00:19:31.020 --> 00:19:34.510
you achieve economy of
scale with shared use.

00:19:34.510 --> 00:19:36.810
So, in the beginning,
that shared use

00:19:36.810 --> 00:19:39.010
did provide some challenges.

00:19:39.010 --> 00:19:42.300
So we overcame this
lopsided nature

00:19:42.300 --> 00:19:44.220
of some of the larger
customer resource

00:19:44.220 --> 00:19:46.830
allocations by segmenting
the architecture

00:19:46.830 --> 00:19:51.240
with separate hosted instances
of Google BigQuery for larger

00:19:51.240 --> 00:19:52.507
service providers.

00:19:52.507 --> 00:19:53.340
FILLIPO MADELLA: OK.

00:19:53.340 --> 00:19:58.170
And if you were to do
any future enhancements

00:19:58.170 --> 00:20:04.410
to your architecture, do you
have any plans or something

00:20:04.410 --> 00:20:07.020
that you could share with
those watching a little bit?

00:20:07.020 --> 00:20:08.923
What's something that
is on your roadmap?

00:20:08.923 --> 00:20:09.840
EDWIN MARGULIES: Yeah.

00:20:09.840 --> 00:20:12.780
I would say that we'll
continue to expand

00:20:12.780 --> 00:20:18.078
our suite of AI-based
services based on Google AI.

00:20:18.078 --> 00:20:22.930
At present, we offer Google Data
Loss Protection, Dialogflow,

00:20:22.930 --> 00:20:26.970
AI-based classification,
Google Sentiment Analysis,

00:20:26.970 --> 00:20:30.680
Google Speech to
Text, Text to Speech,

00:20:30.680 --> 00:20:32.870
Google Natural Language.

00:20:32.870 --> 00:20:36.710
So our focus in 2022 will be
more enhancements to Google

00:20:36.710 --> 00:20:41.240
Agent Assist technology to
augment our own robotic process

00:20:41.240 --> 00:20:41.977
automation.

00:20:41.977 --> 00:20:42.810
FILLIPO MADELLA: OK.

00:20:42.810 --> 00:20:46.160
Certainly a lot of our
menu there included.

00:20:46.160 --> 00:20:52.160
And, just to wrap it up,
how can people watching

00:20:52.160 --> 00:20:53.700
learn more about Thrio?

00:20:53.700 --> 00:20:55.010
EDWIN MARGULIES: OK, sure.

00:20:55.010 --> 00:20:57.320
Well, the best place
to go is thrio.com.

00:20:57.320 --> 00:21:01.790
That's T-H-R-I-O. And there's
a lot of information in there

00:21:01.790 --> 00:21:05.150
about how we use Google and
all the applications that we

00:21:05.150 --> 00:21:06.050
provide.

00:21:06.050 --> 00:21:07.425
FILLIPO MADELLA:
You just saw how

00:21:07.425 --> 00:21:10.400
Thrio is able to host global
applications with ease using

00:21:10.400 --> 00:21:13.970
GKE, to store and analyze
millions of records

00:21:13.970 --> 00:21:17.540
using Google BigQuery, and also
provide an intelligent contact

00:21:17.540 --> 00:21:20.870
center as a service,
all powered by Google AI

00:21:20.870 --> 00:21:25.900
to serve their global customers
and BPO needs around the world.

00:21:25.900 --> 00:21:27.670
If you found this
video helpful and you

00:21:27.670 --> 00:21:31.210
want to watch more "Architecting
with Google Cloud" videos,

00:21:31.210 --> 00:21:33.460
please make sure that
you like this video

00:21:33.460 --> 00:21:36.480
and subscribe to
our YouTube channel.

